Created the separate 27-page v0.5 draft: :codex-file-citation{path="C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Referee Repairs 2026-09-29/Observation_Refinement_Descent_and_Cone_Draft_v0.5.pdf" purpose="output"}.

It fixes Theorem 2.4’s fixed-arity proof and one-factor exception, states sufficient subcomplex-cover and site hypotheses for Theorem 11.2, narrows what cone homology is said to detect, and clarifies the prior-art boundary. I also added the missing hypersimplex proof step and explicit signed paths for the seven-state \(d^2\) witness. The [point-by-point referee response](<C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Referee Repairs 2026-09-29/RESPONSE_TO_REFEREE.md>) and [signed-witness supplement](<C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Referee Repairs 2026-09-29/SIGNED_WITNESS_SUPPLEMENT.md>) accompany it.

Verification passed: the v0.4 source and all 83 transferred manifest files match their hashes; targeted Boolean checks pass; LaTeX reports no warnings or bad boxes; and all 27 rendered pages passed visual review. I did not repeat the earlier interaction enumeration because these repairs do not change its results. The [verification receipt](<C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Referee Repairs 2026-09-29/VERIFICATION_RECEIPT.json>) records the scope.

**Next priority is human mathematical and novelty review** of v0.5 before submission. Resolve the companion manuscript’s author attribution as part of that review. This manuscript work can stay in this task; Sol with high reasoning is suitable for subsequent bounded edits. No model run is needed while awaiting human feedback.

- :codex-followup[Prepare human review packet]{prompt="Prepare a local packet for human mathematical review of v0.5, its referee response, signed-witness supplement, and priority questions. Do not send it externally."}
- :codex-followup[Resolve author attribution]{prompt="Inspect the manuscript and archived companion author attributions and prepare the exact publication-facing correction for my review."}
- :codex-followup[Draft focused submission version]{prompt="Create a separate focused submission draft from v0.5 centered on the carrier-splitting and interaction results, preserving all established limitations and earlier versions."}

Completed the convergence review and created the separate, integrated 27-page v0.4 manuscript: :codex-file-citation{path="C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Convergence 2026-09-29/Observation_Refinement_Descent_and_Cone_Draft_v0.4.pdf" purpose="output"}.

- Integrated the matching and interaction-core results, preserving counterexamples and priority caveats.
- Corrected the descent theorem: reconstruction on \(Y\subset X\) does not imply unique extension to \(X\).
- Preserved the frozen v0.3 and all 83 transferred files; hashes match.
- Reproduced both integral \(d^2\) certificates and the torsion example. Compilation and visual checks passed.

The [review and correction record](<C:/Users/brian/Documents/Math Latex etc/Papers for Publication/The MIND/Manuscript Convergence 2026-09-29/CONVERGENCE_REVIEW.md>) explains the remaining publication boundaries.

I recommend independent expert review before another research gate. Editorial preparation belongs in this task; GPT-6 Sol with high reasoning should suffice. No further run is required to complete this phase.

- :codex-followup[Prepare an expert-review packet]{prompt="Prepare a local expert-review packet for v0.4 emphasizing the main claims, priority questions, counterexamples, and descent correction. Do not send it externally."}
- :codex-followup[Explain the descent correction]{prompt="Explain the v0.3 descent error and its v0.4 correction, including what changes mathematically and what remains valid."}
- :codex-followup[Plan a focused submission]{prompt="Propose a focused submission outline centered on the carrier-splitting and interaction theorems, preserving v0.4 and identifying material suitable for appendices."}
